{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UQQAmGu8xHsrUnPJX28QRq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-31T10:26:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs) — CC BY 4.0, The Kubernetes Authors\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D2 Security\", \"D2.2\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"pod-security-standards\", \"pss-privileged\", \"pss-baseline\", \"pss-restricted\", \"run-as-non-root\", \"run-as-user\", \"allow-privilege-escalation\", \"linux-capabilities\", \"privileged-container\", \"seccomp\", \"apparmor\", \"policy-engine\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Pod Security Standards — the profile control tables (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/)\n9\t\n10\tCompanion to `k8s-docs-pod-security-standards-2026-08-23.md`, which carries the summary table and the three profile descriptions. This snapshot carries the per-control detail that `ch12-fig04` needs: the specific `securityContext` fields each level constrains and the values each permits.\n11\t\n12\t## Summary table (repeated for context)\n13\t\n14\t| Profile | Description |\n15\t|---|---|\n16\t| Privileged | \"Unrestricted policy, providing the widest possible level of permissions.\" |\n17\t| Baseline | \"Minimally restrictive policy which prevents known privilege escalations.\" |\n18\t| Restricted | \"Heavily restricted policy, following current Pod hardening best practices.\" |\n19\t\n20\t## Privileged\n21\t\n22\t\"The _Privileged_ policy is purposely-open, and entirely unrestricted.\" Targeted at system- and infrastructure-level workloads managed by privileged, trusted users. A Pod under it is able to bypass typical container isolation mechanisms — for example, accessing the node's host network.\n23\t\n24\t## Baseline\n25\t\n26\t\"The _Baseline_ policy is aimed at ease of adoption for common containerized workloads while preventing known privilege escalations.\"\n27\t\n28\t### Baseline controls, with the fields restricted and the values allowed"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"07ea436a-2902-492c-8687-6fe3bb13fd7e","uuid":"457a8fc9-0f26-439a-815c-2c586d2dbf3e","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:00:21.996Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-pod-security-standards-profiles-2026-08-31.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-31T10:26:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs) — CC BY 4.0, The Kubernetes Authors\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D2 Security\", \"D2.2\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"pod-security-standards\", \"pss-privileged\", \"pss-baseline\", \"pss-restricted\", \"run-as-non-root\", \"run-as-user\", \"allow-privilege-escalation\", \"linux-capabilities\", \"privileged-container\", \"seccomp\", \"apparmor\", \"policy-engine\"]\n---\n# Pod Security Standards — the profile control tables (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/)\n\nCompanion to `k8s-docs-pod-security-standards-2026-08-23.md`, which carries the summary table and the three profile descriptions. This snapshot carries the per-control detail that `ch12-fig04` needs: the specific `securityContext` fields each level constrains and the values each permits.\n\n## Summary table (repeated for context)\n\n| Profile | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Privileged | \"Unrestricted policy, providing the widest possible level of permissions.\" |\n| Baseline | \"Minimally restrictive policy which prevents known privilege escalations.\" |\n| Restricted | \"Heavily restricted policy, following current Pod hardening best practices.\" |\n\n## Privileged\n\n\"The _Privileged_ policy is purposely-open, and entirely unrestricted.\" Targeted at system- and infrastructure-level workloads managed by privileged, trusted users. A Pod under it is able to bypass typical container isolation mechanisms — for example, accessing the node's host network.\n\n## Baseline\n\n\"The _Baseline_ policy is aimed at ease of adoption for common containerized workloads while preventing known privilege escalations.\"\n\n### Baseline controls, with the fields restricted and the values allowed","numLines":28,"startLine":1,"totalLines":102}}}
